all right let's do some audience  questions we have some really really  good questions this week the first of  which is from Peter Peter said I've  heard you recommend some behavioral  economics books but I know your  background is in traditional economic  can you talk about where you think  reality falls between so-called rational  economics and behavioral economics okay  brilliant brilliant question a  traditional classical rational economics  assumes that individuals and markets are  perfectly and completely rational and  not influenced by cognitive biases by  Framing  by all sorts of other elements which  would lead to non rational decisions so  this would include for example some some  classic examples of this would be if you  ask someone you have two options you  have two bets that you can take you can  have a 52% chance to win $100 and a 48%  chance to win 0 or you can have a 100%  chance to win $50 right the rational  decision would be if you consider the  expected value of that bet would be to  take the 52 percent chance of winning  $100 because the expected value of that  bet is 52 dollars right this is just  simple a probability theory the expected  value of a 52% chance of winning a  hundred bucks and a 48% chance of  winning zero is fifty two dollars per  bet obviously when you have one bet you  will either win a hundred or win 0 but  over time the expected value is $52 the  expected value of a 50% of a hundred  percent chance to win $50 is $50  therefore a perfectly rational actor who  is not influenced by cognitive biases  and heuristics as described by people  like Daniel Kahneman in the book  Thinking Fast and Slow would take the 52  percent chance of winning a hundred  bucks but most of us realize that we  wouldn't do that I would probably just  take the $50 sure bet yeah and  especially  because it doesn't really provide any  insight as to what your financial  situation is so if you're in need of  money then you take the Surefire $50  rather than taking a little bit more  than 50/50 chance at 100 bucks that is  another aspect where behavioral  economics which seeks to address how  people actually make decisions versus  this hypothetical rational actor another  one of the examples that that behavioral  economics has given us is the idea that  money the same amount of money has a  different utility to us depending not  only on our net worth at the time a  hundred bucks has a different value  depending on what your net worth is and  thus you would not actually see the same  decisions made about a hundred dollars  by everybody which would counteract the  totally rational actor what also matters  is whether you're talking about a gain  or a loss of that amount and Daniel  Kahneman's groundbreaking work into loss  aversion is also another way in which we  have seen that behavioral economics  reflects a much more real-world  interpretation of rationality and  so-called rational economic now these  are all super specific examples right  and I encourage you to sort of read and  I'll probably recommend soon Daniel  Daniel Kahneman's book but my view is  the following big picture ok without  just digging into specific examples I  think it's really important as a base to  teach rational neoclassical economics  because in order to again have a better  understanding of how people really  operate it's good to know here's what  the world would look like if people were  completely rational as traditional  economics described it would be more  tough now when I pick up a behavioral  economics book I have a much better  understanding of it because I understand  that this is in comparison to theories  of rational economics if I just jumped  into behavioral economics I would not  necessarily understand why a lot of that  writing is so important and relevant so  I am completely in favor of teaching and  I started my education in economics with  traditional India  classical rational actor Mart stock  prices reflect all known information  blah-blah-blah-blah-blah that that was a  great baseline form but my personal  beliefs now are much more in line with  the behavioral economics work of people  like Daniel Kahneman and Dan Ariely yeah  it's important to keep in mind that  while we may be logical beings to a  great extent I think honestly we're more  impacted by emotions than anything else  so emotions make you feel one way or  another that could completely cook  influenced you to act in a way that's  just not rational there are so many  examples of how framing affects  decision-making if we were perfectly  rational framing would not affect  decision-making and what I mean is for  example people who are told to spin the  roulette wheel okay and after spinning  the roulette wheel they are asked  whether they would pay that amount of  money that came up on the roulette wheel  for a bottle of wine or another example  is with you take your last two digits of  your social security number which are  random right and after talk and then  you're asked would you pay that amount  of money for a bottle of wine and it's  the same bottle of wine those whose last  two digits of their social security  number are a bigger number are willing  to pay more for the same bottle of wine  as people have a social security number  that ended lower numbers there is no  rational basis for that that is priming  or framing and there these are endless  examples so I don't believe for a second  that the rational actors of traditional  economics exist but I think it's  perfectly fine to teach that to start a  foundation for an understanding of  decision-making in the world really  really good question

